<div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>What an insurance estimate review is</h2>
<p>Most carrier estimates are written in Xactimate or similar software. They look precise, but they are built from a scope someone chose and a price list someone selected. An insurance estimate review checks both. We compare the estimate against the photos, measurements, your contractor’s bid and the policy, and tell you in writing what’s missing, what’s under-priced and roughly what the gap is worth.</p>
<h2>What we look for</h2>
<ul>
<li>Missing rooms, slopes, elevations or trades.</li>
<li>Quantities that don’t match the measurements.</li>
<li>Unit prices that don’t reflect the local market.</li>
<li>Repair-versus-replace decisions that ignore matching or code.</li>
<li>Overhead and profit omitted on a multi-trade job.</li>
<li>Depreciation applied to labor or at unreasonable rates.</li>
<li>Sales tax, permits, dumpsters and cleanup.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What happens next</h2>
<p>Often the review is enough. A well-documented supplement request to the adjuster closes many gaps. California’s Fair Claims Settlement Practices Regulations require insurers to respond to claim communications within set time frames, which helps keep a supplement moving. If the gap remains, the review becomes the foundation of an appraisal valuation.</p>
<h3>When to get one</h3>
<ul>
<li>When you receive the first estimate and it looks low.</li>
<li>Before you sign any release or accept a “final” payment.</li>
<li>Before you invoke appraisal, so you know what you are asking the panel to decide.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use our <a href="/claim-check-tools/appraisal-payoff-estimator/">appraisal payoff estimator</a> to see whether the gap you already know about is likely to be worth the cost of appraisal.</p>

<h2>Questions about insurance estimate review</h2><div class="qa"><details><summary>How much does an estimate review cost?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>We quote a flat fee in writing before starting, based on the size of the claim. The first conversation is free.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do I need my own estimate first?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>No. We can work from the carrier’s estimate and your photos, and inspect if needed.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can I send the review to my adjuster?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Yes. Many clients use it to support a supplement request.</p></div></details></div>
